],
"text": "[curious] Start with the upside-down trick, because it's the cleverest. Under that ramp, gravity pulls the car away with its full weight. That's one g. To stay put, something has to push back harder. Tesla says fans pull air out from under the car. That leaves a thin layer of low-pressure air between the floor and the ramp, while the air on the other side stays at normal pressure\u2026 and normal air pressure is surprisingly strong. The difference shoves the car into the surface. Tesla claims the system can pull up to two g: double what it needs to hang there. Racing teams tried fan cars back in the 1970s, and they gripped so well that the rules shut them down. And notice: this suction is a separate system from the jets.",
